import Checks.Universality.RecursionFree.DecompositionReview
import InteractionCombinators.Universality.RecursionFree.DecompositionSymm
import InteractionCombinators.Universality.RecursionFree.SymmPerm

/-!
# Review of the symmetric decomposition (page 86)

Review of `Rule.exists_symm_decomposition` (`Lafont/DecompositionSymm.lean`) and of its literal
case `Rule.SymmLit.exists_symm_decomposition` (`Lafont/DecompositionSymmLiteral.lean`) against
Lafont 1997, pages 85–86 (PDF pages 17–18) and Fig. 7. Issue #132.

Page 85: "By the symmetry condition, and by uniqueness of the canonical decomposition, one gets
`φ_{j,i} = ψ_{i,j}` and `σ_{j,i} = σ_{i,j}⁻¹`. In particular, if `i = j`, one gets
`σ_{i,i} = σ_{i,i}⁻¹`, which means that `σ_{i,i}` is involutive." Page 86: "To sum up, one can
assume that in all cases, `φ_{j,i} = ψ_{i,j}`, `σ_{j,i} = σ_{i,j}`, and `σ_{i,j}` is involutive."
At `i = j` the first equation is `ψ_{i,i} = φ_{i,i}`. The last row of Fig. 7 plugs `[φ_{i,j}]` and
`[φ_{j,i}]` through `σ_{i,j}` and calls the result `[ν_{i,j}]`.

## The checks

* The conclusion says what the page says: `D.ψ = D.φ`, `D.σ (D.σ l) = l`, and the plug
  `Rule.plug D.φ D.ψ D.σ` is `r` up to `Equiv` and an injective renaming of the internal wires.
  It gives also `D.φ.Linear` and `D.φ.Reduced` (`φ_{i,j}` is a reduced net, page 85).
* The hypotheses are exactly those of `System.WellFormed` for a rule `α`–`α`
  (`exists_symm_decomposition_of_wellFormed`).
* They are not vacuous: `DecompositionSymmCheck` already applies the theorem to `con`–`con`,
  `dup`–`dup`, `era`–`era`, and to `selfRule` of `DecompositionReview`, which is symmetric only
  up to `Equiv` and whose canonical `σ` is the 3-cycle `[2, 0, 1, 3]`. Those instances are not
  repeated here; `exists_symm_decomposition_of_wellFormed` applies it to every rule `α`–`α` of
  any well-formed system, `Comb.system` included (`system_wellFormed`).
* `con`–`con` (Fig. 2, the annihilation that exchanges the auxiliary ports): the canonical
  decomposition is already symmetric, `ψ = φ` (`conCon_ψ_eq_φ`), with `σ` the exchange `[1, 0]`
  (`DecompositionCheck.conCon_σ`), an involution other than the identity. For that `φ`, the
  identity does not give back `con`–`con` (`conCon_plug_refl_not`): plugging through the identity
  is `dup`–`dup`. So `σ` carries the crossing of the rule. `dup`–`dup` has `ψ = φ` and `σ = 1`.
* The renaming `ι` cannot hide a degenerate plug. `Equiv` keeps cells and free ports, so the
  conclusion forces the cells of `r` to be those of `φ`, twice (`symm_decomposition_cells`), and on
  `con`–`con` it pins the wiring of the ports (`conCon_plug_refl_not`).

## A defect: `System.WellFormed.symm` is stronger than the paper's symmetry condition

Page 73 asks, for a symbol interacting with itself, `ν̄_{i,i} = ν_{i,i}`: the net read from the
other cell is *the same net*, that is the same graph. `System.WellFormed.symm` asks
`Equiv r.swap.net r.net`. `Equiv` is generated by wire substitutions over the names of the rule;
it cannot rename an internal wire unless some equation `x = w` frees a name.

`twin` is a rule of a symbol `α` of arity 1 with itself: `a = β(w₀, w₁)`, `b = β(w₁, w₀)`, for a
binary symbol `β`. It is linear and reduced, and read from the other cell it is the same net with
`w₀` and `w₁` exchanged (`twin_swap`): it satisfies the paper's condition. But it is not `Equiv`
to its swap (`twin_not_symm`), because neither net admits a wire substitution in either direction
(`Net.equiv_eq_of_rigid`). So the system `sysT` with the single rule `α`–`α` given by `twin` is an
interaction system in the sense of page 73. When this review was written, `System.WellFormed.symm`
asked for `Equiv r.swap.net r.net`, and `sysT` was therefore not well-formed, so that the target
theorem said nothing about it. The owner has since changed the condition to symmetry up to a
permutation of the internal wires (issue #136); `sysT` is now well-formed
(`sysT_wellFormed` in `Checks/SymmPermCheck.lean`).
-/

namespace INet.Comb.DecompositionSymmReview

open Term Rule DecompositionReview DecompositionCheck

/-! ### The hypotheses are those of a well-formed system -/

/-- For a well-formed interaction system, every rule `α`–`α` has the symmetric decomposition of
page 86: the hypotheses of `Rule.exists_symm_decomposition` are exactly the fields `linear`,
`reduced` and `symm` of `System.WellFormed`. -/
theorem exists_symm_decomposition_of_wellFormed {S : Signature} {I : System S} (hI : I.WellFormed)
    (α : S.Sym) (r : Rule S (S.arity α) (S.arity α)) (h : I.rule α α = some r) :
    ∃ D : Decomposition S (S.arity α) (S.arity α), D.ψ = D.φ ∧ (∀ l, D.σ (D.σ l) = l) ∧
      D.φ.Linear ∧ D.φ.Reduced ∧
      ∃ ι : Fin r.internal → Fin (Rule.plug D.φ D.ψ D.σ).internal, Function.Injective ι ∧
        Equiv (Rule.plug D.φ D.ψ D.σ).net
          (r.net.bind fun x => var (Sum.map id (Sum.map id ι) x)) :=
  r.exists_symm_decomposition_of_symmPerm (hI.linear α α r h) (hI.reduced α α r h) (hI.symm α r h)

/-! ### `con`–`con`: which `σ` -/

/-- The canonical decomposition of `con`–`con` is already symmetric: `ψ = φ` (page 85). With
`DecompositionCheck.conCon_σ`, its `σ` is the exchange `[1, 0]`, an involution. -/
theorem conCon_ψ_eq_φ :
    (show Net sig (Fin 2 ⊕ Fin 2 ⊕ Fin 0) from ruleConCon.decompose.ψ.net) =
      (show Net sig (Fin 2 ⊕ Fin 2 ⊕ Fin 0) from ruleConCon.decompose.φ.net) := by
  decide

/-- The canonical decomposition of `dup`–`dup` is symmetric with `σ = 1`: `ψ = φ` and
`σ = [0, 1]` (page 85). -/
theorem dupDup_canonical :
    (show Net sig (Fin 2 ⊕ Fin 2 ⊕ Fin 0) from ruleDupDup.decompose.ψ.net) =
      (show Net sig (Fin 2 ⊕ Fin 2 ⊕ Fin 0) from ruleDupDup.decompose.φ.net) ∧
    (List.finRange ruleDupDup.decompose.k).map (fun l => (ruleDupDup.decompose.σ l).val) =
      [0, 1] := by
  decide

/-- For the side `φ` of `con`–`con` (`a = 0`, `b = 1`), plugging `φ` with itself through the
identity does not give back `con`–`con`, for any renaming: it joins the port `a` of the first
cell to the port `a` of the second (`dup`–`dup`), while `con`–`con` joins `a` to `b` (Fig. 2). So
the involution `σ` of the symmetric decomposition carries the crossing of the rule, and it cannot
be the identity here (page 86, Fig. 7). -/
theorem conCon_plug_refl_not : ¬ ∃ ι : Fin ruleConCon.internal →
    Fin (Rule.plug ruleConCon.decompose.φ ruleConCon.decompose.φ (_root_.Equiv.refl _)).internal,
    Function.Injective ι ∧
      Equiv (Rule.plug ruleConCon.decompose.φ ruleConCon.decompose.φ (_root_.Equiv.refl _)).net
        (ruleConCon.net.bind fun x => var (Sum.map id (Sum.map id ι) x)) := by
  rintro ⟨ι, -, hE⟩
  let s : Fin 2 ⊕ Fin 2 ⊕ Fin (Rule.plug ruleConCon.decompose.φ ruleConCon.decompose.φ
      (_root_.Equiv.refl _)).internal → Term sig (Fin 2) :=
    Sum.elim (fun i => var i) (Sum.elim (fun j => var (j + 1)) fun _ => var 0)
  have hN : Net.Sol (ruleConCon.net.bind fun x => var (Sum.map id (Sum.map id ι) x)) s := by
    refine ⟨s, ?_, fun _ _ => rfl⟩
    intro e he
    obtain ⟨e₀, he₀, rfl⟩ := Multiset.mem_map.1 he
    have key : ∀ e ∈ ruleConCon.net, e = s(pl 0, pr 1) ∨ e = s(pl 1, pr 0) := by
      decide
    rcases key e₀ he₀ with rfl | rfl <;> rfl
  obtain ⟨s', hs, hp⟩ := (hE.sol_iff s).2 hN
  have h1 := hs s(var (.inl 0), var (.inr (.inr ⟨0, by decide⟩))) (by decide)
  have h2 := hs s(var (.inr (.inl 0)), var (.inr (.inr ⟨0, by decide⟩))) (by decide)
  have p1 := hp (.inl 0) (by decide)
  have p2 := hp (.inr (.inl 0)) (by decide)
  simp only [Sym2.lift_mk, Term.bind_var] at h1 h2
  rw [p1] at h1
  rw [p2, ← h1] at h2
  exact absurd h2 (by decide)

/-! ### The renaming `ι` is not degenerate -/

/-- The conclusion of `Rule.exists_symm_decomposition` determines the cells: the cells of `r` are
those of `φ`, twice. `Equiv` keeps the cells (`Equiv.cells_eq`), and renaming wires changes none,
so the injective renaming `ι` cannot make the `Equiv` hold trivially (page 86, last row of
Fig. 7). -/
theorem symm_decomposition_cells {S : Signature} {a : ℕ} {r : Rule S a a}
    {D : Decomposition S a a} (hψ : D.ψ = D.φ)
    {ι : Fin r.internal → Fin (Rule.plug D.φ D.ψ D.σ).internal}
    (heq : Equiv (Rule.plug D.φ D.ψ D.σ).net
      (r.net.bind fun x => var (Sum.map id (Sum.map id ι) x))) :
    D.φ.net.cells + D.φ.net.cells = r.net.cells := by
  have h := heq.cells_eq
  rw [Net.cells_bind_var] at h
  rw [← h]
  have hadd : ∀ {ν : Type} (N M : Net S ν), Net.cells (N + M) = N.cells + M.cells :=
    fun N M => Multiset.add_bind _ _ _
  simp only [Rule.plug]
  rw [hadd, Net.cells_bind_var, Net.cells_bind_var, hψ]

end INet.Comb.DecompositionSymmReview

/-! ### `System.WellFormed.symm` is stronger than the paper's symmetry condition -/

namespace INet

open Term

/-- A net in which every name occurs, and in which no name at the root of an equation `x = t`
occurs twice, is `Equiv` only to itself: no wire substitution starts from it (its wire would occur
twice), and none ends at it (the eliminated name would no longer occur). -/
theorem Net.equiv_eq_of_rigid {S : Signature} {ν : Type} [DecidableEq ν] {N M : Net S ν}
    (hall : ∀ y, y ∈ N.vars) (hroot : ∀ x t, s(var x, t) ∈ N → N.vars.count x ≤ 1)
    (h : Equiv N M) : M = N := by
  have hout : ∀ X, ¬ Wire N X := by
    intro X hw
    cases hw with
    | mk x t a b Δ hlin hx =>
      have h1 := hroot x t (Multiset.mem_cons_self _ _)
      have h2 := Multiset.one_le_count_iff_mem.2 hx
      simp only [Net.vars_cons, eqVars_mk, Term.vars_var, Multiset.count_add,
        Multiset.count_singleton_self] at h1 h2
      omega
  have hin : ∀ X, ¬ Wire X N := by
    intro X hw
    cases hw with
    | mk x t a b Δ hlin hx =>
      have hc := hlin x
      have hx1 := Multiset.one_le_count_iff_mem.2 hx
      have hy := Multiset.one_le_count_iff_mem.2 (hall x)
      simp only [Net.vars_cons, eqVars_mk, Term.vars_var, Multiset.count_add,
        Multiset.count_singleton_self] at hc hx1
      have ht : t.vars.count x = 0 := by omega
      simp only [Net.vars_cons, eqVars_mk, Multiset.count_add, Term.count_vars_subst, ht,
        mul_zero, ite_true, add_zero, zero_add] at hy
      omega
  have key : ∀ X Y, Relation.EqvGen Wire X Y → (X = N ↔ Y = N) := by
    intro X Y hXY
    induction hXY with
    | rel X Y hw =>
      constructor
      · rintro rfl; exact absurd hw (hout _)
      · rintro rfl; exact absurd hw (hin _)
    | refl => exact Iff.rfl
    | symm _ _ _ ih => exact ih.symm
    | trans _ _ _ _ _ ih₁ ih₂ => exact ih₁.trans ih₂
  exact (key N M h).1 rfl

namespace DecompositionSymmReview

/-- Two symbols: `α` with one auxiliary port, `β` with two. -/
inductive TSym : Type
  | α | β
  deriving DecidableEq

/-- The signature of `TSym`. -/
def sigT : Signature where
  Sym := TSym
  arity
    | .α => 1
    | .β => 2

instance : DecidableEq sigT.Sym := inferInstanceAs (DecidableEq TSym)

instance {ν : Type} [DecidableEq ν] : DecidableEq (Term sigT ν) := Comb.DecompositionCheck.termDecEq

/-- A `β` cell with trees on its auxiliary ports. -/
def mkB {ν : Type} (u v : Term sigT ν) : Term sigT ν := .app TSym.β ![u, v]

/-- An internal wire of a rule `α`–`α`. -/
abbrev tw (w : Fin 2) : Term sigT (Fin 1 ⊕ Fin 1 ⊕ Fin 2) := var (.inr (.inr w))

/-- The rule `α`–`α` with right member `a = β(w₀, w₁)`, `b = β(w₁, w₀)`, where `a` and `b` are the
auxiliary ports of the two `α` cells: two `β` cells whose auxiliary ports are crossed. -/
def twin : Rule sigT 1 1 :=
  ⟨2, s(var (.inl 0), mkB (tw 0) (tw 1)) ::ₘ {s(var (.inr (.inl 0)), mkB (tw 1) (tw 0))}⟩

/-- `twin` is linear. -/
theorem twin_linear : twin.Linear := ⟨by decide, by decide, by decide⟩

/-- `twin` is reduced: both equations are rooted at a port. -/
theorem twin_reduced : twin.Reduced := by
  intro e he
  dsimp only [twin] at he
  rcases Multiset.mem_cons.1 he with rfl | he
  · exact ⟨.inl 0, _, rfl, fun w h => by cases h⟩
  obtain rfl := Multiset.mem_singleton.1 he
  exact ⟨.inr (.inl 0), _, rfl, fun w h => by cases h⟩

/-- `twin` satisfies the symmetry condition of page 73 (`ν̄_{i,i} = ν_{i,i}` as graphs): read from
the other cell, it is the same net with the internal wires `w₀` and `w₁` exchanged. -/
theorem twin_swap : (show Net sigT (Fin 1 ⊕ Fin 1 ⊕ Fin 2) from twin.swap.net) =
    (show Net sigT (Fin 1 ⊕ Fin 1 ⊕ Fin 2) from twin.net).bind
      fun x => var (Sum.map id (Sum.map id (_root_.Equiv.swap (0 : Fin 2) 1)) x) := by
  decide

/-- `twin` read from the other cell is not literally `twin`. -/
theorem twin_swap_ne : (show Net sigT (Fin 1 ⊕ Fin 1 ⊕ Fin 2) from twin.swap.net) ≠
    (show Net sigT (Fin 1 ⊕ Fin 1 ⊕ Fin 2) from twin.net) := by
  decide

/-- `twin` does not satisfy `System.WellFormed.symm`: it is not `Equiv` to its swap. Every name of
`twin` occurs, and the roots of its equations are ports, which occur once, so `twin` admits no
wire substitution in either direction (`Net.equiv_eq_of_rigid`). -/
theorem twin_not_symm : ¬ Equiv twin.swap.net twin.net := by
  intro h
  refine twin_swap_ne (Net.equiv_eq_of_rigid (N := twin.net) (fun y => ?_) ?_ h.symm)
  · revert y
    decide
  · intro x t hm
    dsimp only [twin] at hm
    rcases Multiset.mem_cons.1 hm with e | hm
    · rcases Sym2.eq_iff.1 e with ⟨e1, -⟩ | ⟨e1, -⟩
      · cases e1
        decide
      · cases e1
    · rcases Sym2.eq_iff.1 (Multiset.mem_singleton.1 hm) with ⟨e1, -⟩ | ⟨e1, -⟩
      · cases e1
        decide
      · cases e1

/-- The system with the single rule `twin` for `α`–`α`. It is an interaction system in the sense
of page 73: one rule, linear, reduced, and symmetric as a graph (`twin_swap`). -/
def sysT : System sigT where
  rule
    | .α, .α => some twin
    | _, _ => none

/-- The other conditions of `System.WellFormed` hold for `sysT`: every rule is linear and reduced,
and the uniqueness condition holds. -/
theorem sysT_rest :
    (∀ x y r, sysT.rule x y = some r → r.Linear) ∧
    (∀ x y r, sysT.rule x y = some r → r.Reduced) ∧
    (∀ x y, x ≠ y → (sysT.rule x y).isSome → sysT.rule y x = none) := by
  refine ⟨fun x y r h => ?_, fun x y r h => ?_, fun x y hne h => ?_⟩
  · cases x <;> cases y <;> cases h
    exact twin_linear
  · cases x <;> cases y <;> cases h
    exact twin_reduced
  · cases x <;> cases y <;> first | exact absurd rfl hne | cases h

end DecompositionSymmReview

end INet
